// For God so loved the world that he gave his only begotten Son, that whoever believes in him should not perish but have eternal life.
// Linguistic observations from the LLM merge pass — one JSON object per lemma
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5375","glossChirho":"נשׂא lift/carry","sensesChirho":12,"notesChirho":"Stem (binyan) is primary separator. Within Qal, idiomatic collocations create distinct senses: nasa panim (show partiality), nasa avon (forgive sin vs bear guilt — context-dependent), nasa rosh (take census). The forgive vs endure-consequence split is theologically significant."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7760a","glossChirho":"שׂים put/set","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Arabic جَعَلَ vs وَضَعَ cleanly separates 'make into' (transformative) from 'put/place' (physical). The idiom שׂים לב (set heart = pay attention) is a distinct lexicalized expression."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5674a","glossChirho":"עבר pass/cross","sensesChirho":10,"notesChirho":"Spanish cruzar vs pasar, Korean 건너 vs 지나가, Swahili kuvuka vs kupita all independently distinguish 'cross a boundary' from 'pass through'. Hifil proclaim sense (cause decree to pass through) is a specialized idiom."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1980","glossChirho":"הלך go/walk","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Arabic salaka (follow a path) signals the metaphorical conduct sense vs dhahaba (physical going). Hitpael 'walk about' covers intimate companionship walking (Enoch walked with God, Gen 5:22)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8104","glossChirho":"שׁמר keep/guard","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Qal covers both physical guarding and obedient keeping of commandments in one sense — the multilingual glosses don't consistently separate these. Niphal 'beware/take heed' is reflexive self-guarding."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5493","glossChirho":"סור turn aside","sensesChirho":8,"notesChirho":"Hifil splits into physical removal vs moral purging (putting away idols/evil). Hindi utaarī and Korean beot-da (take off garments) mark the garment-removal sub-sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7725","glossChirho":"שׁוב return","sensesChirho":8,"notesChirho":"Arabic تُوبُوا (tawba = repentance) and Swahili tubuni distinguished the Qal repentance sense from physical return. Hifil 'return a word' = answer/reply is an important idiom (Spanish respondió, Swahili kujibu)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5975","glossChirho":"עמד stand","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Arabic roots ث-ب-ت/ب-ق-ي/ص-م-د (endure) vs و-ق-ف (stop) separate the endure and stop sub-senses within Qal. Hindi ठहर/रुक (stop) vs बना रह/टिक (endure) confirmed."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4672","glossChirho":"מצא find","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Arabic أَصَابَ (befall/strike) vs وَجَدَ (find) distinguishes 'calamity finds someone' from intentional finding. يَكْفِي (suffice) catches the rare 'be enough' sense. Niphal articulated participle הַנִּמְצְאִים is a lexicalized quasi-nominal 'those present'."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3427","glossChirho":"ישׁב sit/dwell","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Spanish sentarse vs habitar vs quedarse, Arabic جلس vs سكن vs مكث, Korean 앉 vs 거주 vs 머물 all independently three-way-split sit/dwell/remain. Mega-cluster 104422 (539 occ) needed manual override from sit to dwell."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3045","glossChirho":"ידע know","sensesChirho":13,"notesChirho":"Spanish saber vs conocer and Arabic عَلِمَ vs عَرَفَ distinguish cognitive from relational knowledge. Sexual knowing (euphemism) confirmed across all languages. Pual participle 'acquaintance' is a lexicalized nominal."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3381","glossChirho":"ירד go down","sensesChirho":9,"notesChirho":"Hifil splits into concrete bringing-down vs figurative sending-down (rain/judgment) — Arabic anuzilu vs ukhdi'u, Hindi utaarna vs barsaana. 'Those going down to the pit' (yorde bor) is a formulaic prophetic expression."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5046","glossChirho":"נגד tell","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Arabic أَخْبَرَ (inform) vs يُعْلِنُ (announce) separates tell/report from declare/proclaim. Swahili tangaza (announce publicly) vs ambia (tell) confirmed. Rare senses: denounce, confess, disclose each have distinctive multilingual evidence."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5221","glossChirho":"נכה strike","sensesChirho":11,"notesChirho":"Arabic qatala (kill) vs daraba (hit) and Swahili kuua vs kupiga separate lethal from non-lethal striking. Specialized sub-senses: clap hands, thrust/stab, drive in nail, defeat — each confirmed by distinct target-language verbs."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6965b","glossChirho":"קום rise","sensesChirho":12,"notesChirho":"Arabic yathbutu (be firm) vs yaqumu (rise) separates stand firm/endure from basic arising. Hifil splits 5 ways: raise person, erect object, establish covenant, fulfill promise, rouse from inactivity. Arabic jamuda (congeal) catches the rare physical 'stand still' sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8130","glossChirho":"שׂנא hate","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Sonnet collapsed to 2 senses; Opus found 6. Key: Qal participle 'hater/enemy' is a lexicalized nominal (Arabic عدو, Swahili adui) distinct from finite 'to hate'. Qal passive participle שְׂנוּאָה 'unloved wife' is adjectival/stative vs Nifal processual passive."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G0142","glossChirho":"αἴρω lift/take","sensesChirho":8,"notesChirho":"Sonnet found 3; Opus found 8. Caught nautical 'weigh anchor' (Acts 27), hostile 'Away with him!' (John 19:15), and idiomatic 'keep in suspense' (John 10:24 τὴν ψυχὴν αἴρω). Korean 들어-올리다 (lift up) vs 가져가다 (take away) was diagnostic."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7069","glossChirho":"קנה acquire/buy","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Spanish comprar vs adquirir vs creador separates buy/purchase from acquire/obtain from create/possess. Gen 14:19 Creator/Possessor of heaven: Arabic خالق, Swahili Muumba. Caught qana'/qanah conflation in Deut 32:21 (provoke to jealousy)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2076","glossChirho":"זבח sacrifice","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Swahili kuchinja (slaughter animal) vs kutoa dhabihu (offer sacrifice) was the cleanest discriminator for the slaughter-vs-sacrifice split. Matches HALOT Qal §1 vs §2-5. Could be refined further with syntactic frames (deity recipients, offering objects)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2428","glossChirho":"חיל strength/army","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Arabic provides 5 distinct lexemes mapping to 5 senses: جيش (army), بأس (valor), ثروة (wealth), قوة (strength), فضيلة (virtue). Korean and Swahili independently confirmed all 5 splits. Best example of multilingual evidence resolving polysemy."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2891","glossChirho":"טהר be clean","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Critical Piel split: declarative 'pronounce clean' (priest's speech act, Lev 13-14) vs factitive 'make clean/purify' (actual removal of impurity). This is a real lexicographic distinction within the same stem."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0929","glossChirho":"בהמה beast/cattle","sensesChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Korean 짐승 (wild/generic beast) vs 가축 (domesticated livestock), Spanish bestia vs ganado, Swahili mnyama vs mifugo. Arabic بَهِيمَة covers both — less discriminating here."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G3860","glossChirho":"παραδίδωμι hand over","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Betray (Judas) vs hand over to custody vs give over/surrender vs entrust vs transmit tradition vs yield fruit. 'Transmit tradition' (1 Cor 11:23) is theologically crucial and distinct from betrayal despite same Greek verb. Hindi पकड़वाना (betray) vs सौंपना (hand over) was diagnostic."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1431","glossChirho":"גדל be great/grow","sensesChirho":9,"notesChirho":"Piel splits: 'raise children' (Arabic رَبَّى, Korean 양육하다, Swahili -lea) vs 'magnify/extol' (Arabic عَظَّمَ, Korean 높이다, Swahili -tukuza) — different semantic domains within same stem."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3824","glossChirho":"לבב heart","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"52 of 54 clusters are one sense — glosses entirely uniform. Swahili katikati ya (middle of) vs moyo (heart) caught the rare spatial 'center/midst' sense. One adjectival 'whole/sound' cluster may be a tagging error."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8451","glossChirho":"תורה Torah/law","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Korean 율법 (law) vs 가르침 (teaching) and Swahili sheria vs mafundisho cleanly separate divine Torah from human instruction. Plural תּוֹרֹת morphologically and semantically distinct as individual regulations."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5650","glossChirho":"עבד servant","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Swahili mtumishi (free servant) vs mtumwa (slave/owned person) was the strongest signal for servant/slave distinction. 'Servant of God' honorific (Moses, David) identified by 1cs possessive + comparative preposition pattern."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8179","glossChirho":"שׁער gate","sensesChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Deuteronomic 'your gates' = 'your towns' metonymy identified by 2nd-person possessive suffix concentration on plural form. All other inflections are one sense — physical gate/entrance."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4725","glossChirho":"מקום place","sensesChirho":2,"notesChirho":"51 of 52 clusters are one sense. Swahili nafasi (space/room) vs mahali (place) caught the single spatial-extent sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8210","glossChirho":"שׁפך pour out","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Arabic سَكَبَ (pour liquid) vs سَفَكَ (shed blood) is a lexical split in Arabic mapping to a real Hebrew polysemy. Korean 쌓다 (pile up) caught the siege-ramp sense within the metaphorical group."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2181","glossChirho":"זנה commit harlotry","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Literal sexual harlotry and metaphorical spiritual unfaithfulness (prophetic idiom) were merged in Qal — flagged for refinement. Hifil causative confirmed by Spanish hizo fornicar, Hindi व्यभिचार कराया, Swahili akawafanya wazini."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6031b","glossChirho":"ענה afflict/humble","sensesChirho":10,"notesChirho":"Piel splits: afflict/oppress vs humble/bring low vs violate/ravish (sexual, Deut 22). Hitpael self-affliction = fasting. Caught Psalm 88 superscription debate: Arabic لِلْإِنْشَادِ and Korean 노래하기 위하여 support 'to sing/chant' (possible root ענה III)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G2564","glossChirho":"καλέω call","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Korean 초대하다/초대받다 (invite) vs 부르다 (call/summon) separates hospitality invitation from general summoning. The divine vocation sense (Pauline 'call to salvation') confirmed by Hindi बुलाहट (calling/vocation) and contextual divine-agent framing."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5641","glossChirho":"סתר hide/conceal","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Clean binyan separation: Hifil causative hide (30 occ), Nifal reflexive/passive be hidden (28 occ), Hitpael deliberate self-concealment (3 occ), Piel intensive (1 occ), Pual passive-intensive (1 occ). The Nifal participle נסתרות 'hidden things' (Deut 29:29) functions as a lexicalized nominal."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0341","glossChirho":"אֹיֵב enemy","sensesChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Remarkably uniform — all 69 clusters collapsed to a single sense. This frozen participle (Qal ptcp of root א-י-ב) has fully lexicalized as a noun 'enemy/adversary' with no semantic variation across 276 occurrences. Arabic عدو, Swahili adui, Korean 원수/적 all uniformly 'enemy'."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1320","glossChirho":"בשׂר flesh","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Korean 고기 (meat) vs 육체/살 (body) vs Swahili ngozi (skin) independently triangulate the three senses. Arabic لَحْم (meat) vs جَسَد (body) vs بَشَرَة (skin surface) confirmed. The Levitical skin-inspection sense is context-specific to priestly examination texts."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0376","glossChirho":"אישׁ man","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"The distributive sense 'each one/everyone' (7 occ) is confirmed by Arabic كُلّ واحد and Korean 각각/모든사람. The 'husband' sense (4 occ) triggered by feminine possessive suffixes. Biological sex 'male' vs 'female' (1 occ) is the Gen 1:27 usage."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G1453","glossChirho":"ἐγείρω raise","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Active resurrection vs passive resurrection is the most critical theological split. Arabic أَقَامَ (active causative) vs قامَ (intransitive rise) distinguished these. Korean 살리다 (make alive) vs 살아나다 (come alive) confirmed. The 'wake from sleep' sense (4 occ) is literal rather than resurrection metaphor — Arabic أَيقَظ (awaken)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1818","glossChirho":"דם blood","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"The bloodshed/violence metonymy (often plural דמים) consistently triggers Arabic قَتْل/عُنْف (killing/violence) vs literal دَم (blood). Bloodguilt as juridical liability is confirmed by the formula דמו בראשו 'his blood on his head'. Swahili damu covers both literal and metonymic."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4376","glossChirho":"מכר sell","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Clean binyan split. Participle 'seller' lexicalized as professional role (Ezek 7, Neh 13). Hitpael 'sell oneself' has both literal (debt servitude, Lev 25:47) and figurative (selling oneself to do evil, 1 Kgs 21:20-25) sub-senses within one stem."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G2596","glossChirho":"κατά according to/down","sensesChirho":11,"notesChirho":"Most polysemous Greek preposition in our data. The genitive/accusative case distinction creates the primary split: genitive 'against/down from' vs accusative 'according to/throughout/each'. Korean 대로 (according to) vs 을 향해 (toward) vs 대하여 (against) discriminated well. The fixed expressions κατ' ἰδίαν (privately) and καθ' ὑπερβολήν (exceedingly) are true idioms."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7495","glossChirho":"רפא heal","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Participle 'physician/healer' is substantival (Arabic طبيب, Swahili mganga). Piel splits: medical treatment of persons vs physical repair of objects (walls, altars). The Hitpael reflexive 'get oneself healed' (2 Kgs 5:11, King seeking cure) shows subject initiative — distinct from passive Nifal."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5750","glossChirho":"עוד still/again","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"The 'while still' temporal sense (בְּעוֹד with prepositional בְּ) is structurally distinct — Spanish 'mientras todavía' and Korean '아직 ~하는 동안' confirmed. Arabic أَيْضًا (also/again) vs لا يزال (still/continuing) is the primary sense split signal. The 'since one's existence' (מֵעוֹד) temporal origin is rare but distinctive."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0639","glossChirho":"אַף nose/anger","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Classic body-part→emotion metonymy. Arabic أَنْف (nose) vs غَضَب (anger) is the cleanest discriminator. The dual אַפַּיִם serves triple duty: nostrils (literal), face (prostration idiom), and slow-to-anger (אֶרֶךְ אַפַּיִם). Arabic الأَناة 'patience/forbearance' catches the idiom. The 'double/twofold' sense (Isa 40:2) is a hapax use of the dual."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G5100","glossChirho":"τὶς someone/anyone","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Arabic أَحَدٌ (person) vs شَيْئًا (thing) vs بَعْضُ (some/plural) cleanly triangulates the core 3-way split. The adjectival/modifier sense is distinguished by Arabic وَاحِدٌ/أَيُّ and Swahili fulani/mmoja. Single interrogative use (John?) may be an accentual overlap with τίς (G5101)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2534","glossChirho":"חֵמָה wrath/venom","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"True homonymy: 'wrath' (Arabic غَضَب, Swahili ghadhabu/hasira) has zero gloss overlap with 'venom' (Arabic سُمّ, Swahili sumu, Hindi ज़هر). The 'heat' sense (Arabic حَرارَة, Swahili joto) may etymologically underlie both extensions (heat of anger, heat of poison) but is semantically distinct. BDB lists these as separate roots."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0802","glossChirho":"אִשָּׁה woman/wife","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Arabic مَرْأَة/امْرَأَة (woman) vs زَوْجَة (wife) and Korean 여자/여인 (woman) vs 아내 (wife) cleanly separate the two main senses. The distributive 'each woman' (אִשָּׁה...רְעוּתָהּ) confirmed by Arabic واحِدَة. Animal 'female/mate' (Gen 7 ark context) caught by Arabic أُنْثَى and Swahili jike."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G3739","glossChirho":"ὅς who/which","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Most clusters merge into one basic relative pronoun sense — the over-splitting was purely grammatical (case/gender/number). Arabic مَنْ (indefinite who) vs الَّذِي (definite who) catches the whoever/whatever generalizing sense. The correlative ὃς μέν...ὃς δέ pattern (Arabic بَعْضٌ, Korean 어떤 것은) is syntactically not a relative clause at all."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5971a","glossChirho":"עַם people","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"98.9% of occurrences are one sense. Arabic عَمِّي (kinsman) vs شَعْبِي (my people) caught the rare familial/ancestral 'kinfolk' sense in death idioms (נֶאֱסַף אֶל-עַמָּיו). Arabic جَماعَة (troop/band) vs شَعْب (people) caught a single military retinue usage."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4639","glossChirho":"מַעֲשֶׂה work/deed","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Arabic صَنْعَة (craftsmanship) vs عَمَل (labor/deed) and Korean 솜씨 (skill) vs 행위 (conduct) vs 일 (work) triangulate 3 main senses. 'Works of God' in doxological context is a theological register distinct from human deeds. Korean 소출 (produce/yield) caught the product/result sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7489a","glossChirho":"רעע be bad/do evil","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Hifil 'do evil' (41 clusters) is the dominant sense, massively over-fragmented by inflection. Hifil participle מְרֵעִים 'evildoers' fully lexicalized as noun (Arabic الأَشْرار, Korean 행악자). Single Nifal passive and single nominal use each have distinctive gloss profiles."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1197a","glossChirho":"בער burn/purge","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"The Deuteronomic purge formula (בִּעַרְתָּ הָרָע) is cleanly separated from physical burning: Arabic تَنْزِع/أَزَالَ (remove) vs أَشْعَلَ/أَوْقَدَ (kindle). Korean 제거하다 (eliminate) vs 태우다 (burn) confirmed. The Hifil causative kindle is kept separate from Piel intensive kindle by stem morphology."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3034","glossChirho":"ידה give thanks/confess","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Caught homonymous Qal root meaning 'shoot/throw' (Arabic ارْمُوا, Korean 쏘라) — entirely different from the Hifil thanksgiving root. Hifil give-thanks (Arabic حَمَدَ) vs Hifil confess-sin (Arabic اِعْتَرَفَ) is a real polysemy within the same stem. Hitpael confession overlaps semantically with Hifil confess but is a distinct morphological voice."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6256","glossChirho":"עֵת time","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Semantically uniform — 47 of 65 clusters are one sense. The כָּעֵת 'about this time/now' construction is semi-grammaticalized (Arabic كَالآنِ = now). The 'occasions/instances' sense caught by Spanish veces, Hindi बार, Korean 번, Swahili mara — all 'times' in frequency sense vs temporal sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G2980","glossChirho":"λαλέω speak","sensesChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Most aggressive merge in the batch — 54 of 67 clusters into one active sense. λαλέω is semantically almost perfectly uniform. Arabic تَكَلَّمَ/يَتَكَلَّمُ never varies. Only the active/passive voice distinction warrants a split (Arabic المَقولُ/قِيلَ for passive). No distinct glossolalia sense emerged — Corinthian tongue-speaking uses the same verb with the same translations."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1984b","glossChirho":"הלל praise/boast/be mad","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Caught homonymous הלל I (be mad, rage) alongside praise/boast. Binyan-based split: Piel praise vs Piel boast vs Pual passive vs Hitpael reflexive boasting. Arabic مَجْنُون (mad) vs يُسَبِّحُ (praise) cleanly separates homonyms. Korean 미치다 vs 찬양하다 likewise. 69→5 senses."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2026","glossChirho":"הרג kill/slay","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Clean morphological split: Qal active finite (kill) vs Qal participle substantival (killer/slayer) vs Qal passive participle (the slain) vs Nifal passive (be killed). 94→4 with massive collapse of inflectional variants. Arabic قَتَلَ (kill) vs قَاتِلٌ (killer) vs مَقْتُولٌ (slain) maps perfectly."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8074","glossChirho":"שׁמם be desolate/appalled","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Dual semantic field: physical desolation (land/cities) vs psychological horror (appalled). Both have causative Hifil counterparts. Arabic خَرِبَ (desolate) vs يَدْهَشُ (be appalled) is cleanest discriminator. Also caught agentive participle 'desolator' and abstract nominal use. 64→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3498","glossChirho":"יתר remain/leave over","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Nifal stative 'remain' vs Nifal participle substantival 'remnant' vs Hifil causative 'leave remaining' vs Hifil 'have abundance/exceed'. The participle-as-noun vs finite-verb distinction is lexicographically significant. Korean 남다 (remain) vs 나머지 (remnant) vs 남기다 (leave over) vs 넘치다 (exceed). 67→4."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G1325","glossChirho":"δίδωμι give","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"General give/bestow vs passive be given vs idiomatic put/place (εἰς τὴν καρδίαν = into the heart) vs yield/produce (of earth/trees) vs give up/hand over. Arabic أَعْطَى vs وُضِعَ vs أَنْتَجَ provides good discrimination. 59→5 with the put/place extension being a genuine Greek idiom."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8354","glossChirho":"שׁתה drink","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Most aggressive merge in batch: 97→3 senses. 91 clusters→1 'drink' sense covering all Qal inflections. Separated substantival participle 'drinker' (5 IDs) and single Nifal passive 'be drunk' (1 ID). Arabic شَرِبَ (drink) vs شَارِبٌ (drinker) confirms the agent-noun split."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5162","glossChirho":"נחם comfort/relent","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Rich Nifal polysemy: 'relent/repent' (Arabic نَدِمَ) vs 'be comforted' (Arabic تَعَزَّى) — same stem, completely different semantics. Hitpael 117244 classified by meaning (relent) not morphology. Compassion sense (يَتَرَحَّمُ) crosses both Nifal and Hitpael. Pual kept separate from Nifal passive per BDB/HALOT. 79→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3384b","glossChirho":"ירה teach/shoot","sensesChirho":8,"notesChirho":"Two fully homonymous roots: ירה I Hifil 'teach' (Arabic عَلَّمَ) vs ירה II Qal 'shoot/throw' (Arabic رَمَى). Teacher participle (מורה) substantivized. Caught rare 'send rain' sense (104053, 104068) — possibly from ירד root. 'Show/point/direct' is genuine extended sense of teach root. Arabic الرُّمَاة (archers) confirms substantival split. 67→8."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0241","glossChirho":"אֹזֶן ear","sensesChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Most aggressive merge in project: 52→1 sense. Pure body-part noun with zero semantic variation across all 6 languages. Metonymic 'hearing/attention' belongs to prepositional constructions (בְּאָזְנֵי), not the noun itself. Arabic أُذُن and مَسَامِع are register variants, not polysemy. Follows BDB/HALOT single-entry treatment."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2490c","glossChirho":"חלל begin/profane","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Two homonymous roots: חלל I Hifil 'begin' (Arabic ابْتَدَأَ) vs חלל II Piel 'profane' (Arabic دَنَّسَ). Caught specialized legal sense 'use fruit/treat as common' (Lev 19, Deut 20) — Arabic يُحَلِّلُهُ 'make permissible' is diagnostic. Hifil causative 'profane/break covenant' kept separate. Passive Nifal/Pual as own sense. 81→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1366","glossChirho":"גְּבוּל border/territory","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Not a pure single-sense noun like H0241. Four genuine senses: border/boundary (geographic line), territory (enclosed area), edge/rim (of altar — Hindi किनारी, Spanish borde), and ordained boundary landmark (wisdom lit./legal prohibition on moving). Arabic حَدّ (line) vs تُخوم (region) cleanly separates senses 1-2. 57→4."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G2192","glossChirho":"ἔχω have","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"91→7 senses. Core 'possess/have' absorbs ~69 clusters. Genuine splits: hold/regard (Arabic يَعتَبِرونَ), physical grasp (Arabic مُتَمَسِّكينَ), frozen participles τῇ ἐχομένῃ 'following day' and τὰς ἐχομένας 'neighboring', pregnancy idiom ἐν γαστρὶ ἔχω (Arabic حَامِلَة), copular state use (καλῶς ἔχω). All 7 match BDAG entries."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0430","glossChirho":"אֱלֹהִים God/gods","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Theologically central lemma. 2599 occ, 56→4 senses. Massive 2179-occ cluster is 'God of Israel' as expected. Clean separation: Arabic اللّٰه (God) vs آلِهَة (gods plural) vs إِلٰه (single foreign god). 'Judge/ruler' sense (Exod 21-22) not distinguishable in cluster data — absorbed into God-of-Israel. 'Divine being' (1 Sam 28:13) captured as separate sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7999a","glossChirho":"שׁלם be complete/repay","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Rich binyan-based polysemy: Piel pay/repay vs fulfill vows vs make restitution, Qal stative 'be complete/at peace', Pual passive, Hifil 'make peace' vs 'complete/finish'. Arabic و-ف-ي (fulfill) vs ج-ز-ي (repay) cleanly separates the two Piel senses. Swahili -lipa (pay) vs -timiza (fulfill) confirms. 70→7."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5771","glossChirho":"עָוֹן iniquity/guilt/punishment","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Classic BDB tripartition (sin/guilt/punishment) confirmed but highly skewed: ~86 of 93 clusters = 'iniquity/sin'. Only 2 clusters clearly = 'guilt' (Swahili hatia diagnostic), 3 = 'punishment' (Korean 벌, Swahili adhabu). Two anomalous singletons: textual variant 'affliction' and possible lemma error. 93→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5869a","glossChirho":"עַיִן eye","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Spring/fountain homonym (Korean 샘/우물) not present in this lemma's data — likely separate lemma ID. Three genuine senses: evaluative 'in the sight of' (~630 occ, dominant), literal eye (body part), and 'appearance/surface' (כְּעֵין construction, Korean 모양/모습 diagnostic). 77→3."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1961","glossChirho":"היה be/become","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Most common Hebrew verb (3576 occ). Conservative merge: copular/existential/narrative NOT split (Arabic كَانَ covers all three). Inchoative 'become' cleanly separated by Arabic صَارَ and Korean 되다. Caught unique Exod 3:14 'I AM' self-revelation sense (Arabic transliterates אֶהְיֶה rather than translating). Nifal stem separate. 97→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8478","glossChirho":"תַּחַת under/instead","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Clean three-way split: spatial 'under' (Arabic تَحْتَ, 24 clusters), substitutionary 'instead of' (Arabic بَدَلَ/عِوَضَ, 39 clusters — includes royal succession formula), causal 'because' (Arabic بِسَبَبِ, 6 clusters). Succession merged with general substitution per BDB. 69→3."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2930a","glossChirho":"טמא be unclean/defile","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Key split: Piel declarative 'pronounce unclean' (priestly, Spanish declarar impuro) vs Piel factitive 'defile/make unclean' (Spanish contaminar). Ritual vs moral defilement NOT split — Arabic نَجَّسَ used for both. Six binyan-based senses. Hitpael 'defile oneself' well-attested in food/dead-body prohibitions. 70→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1869","glossChirho":"דרך tread/bend","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"'Bend the bow' cleanly separated from 'tread' via Arabic شَدَّ/يَنْزِعُ vs دَاسَ, Korean 당기다 vs 밟다. Tread grapes NOT split from general tread — same verbal action, different object. Hifil split: 'lead/guide' (Arabic أَرْشَدَ) vs 'cause to trample/thresh' (Arabic دَرْس). Single-occ 'march/advance' (Arabic دَرَجَ). 56→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6629","glossChirho":"צֹאן flock/sheep","sensesChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Pure collective noun like H0241 (ear). 76→1. All clusters = Arabic غَنَم, Korean 양/양떼, Swahili kondoo. Metaphorical 'flock of God' (Ezek 34) is contextual, not a separate lexical sense. Third single-sense merge in project (after H0341 and H0241)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2403b","glossChirho":"חַטָּאת sin/sin-offering","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Classic BDB tripartition (sin/guilt/punishment) plus two cultic senses. 73 of 92 clusters → 'sin/transgression' (Arabic خَطِيئَة, Korean 죄). Sin-offering (Arabic ذَبِيحَة الخَطِيئَة, Korean 속죄제) is 16 clusters — clearly lexicalized, not contextual. Rare singletons: punishment (Swahili adhabu diagnostic), purification water (Num 19 red heifer), and anomalous 'shame' (Korean 수치, Swahili aibu — possible textual confusion). 92→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1350a","glossChirho":"גאל redeem","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Four distinct substantival participle uses: kinsman-redeemer (Ruth, Arabic وَلِيّ), blood-avenger (Num 35 collocation גֹּאֵל הַדָּם, Arabic وَلِيُّ الدَّمِ), divine Redeemer title (Isaiah 41-63, Arabic فَادِي + possessive), and the-redeemed-ones (passive participle, Arabic مَفْدِيُّونَ). Plus Qal active finite verb 'redeem' and Nifal passive 'be redeemed'. Rich participial polysemy typical of Hebrew verbal system. 60→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6566","glossChirho":"פרשׂ spread out","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Qal 'spread out' absorbs 39 of 51 clusters (hands, wings, nets, garments). Piel bifurcates: intensive 'spread out' vs 'scatter/disperse people' (Arabic فَرَّقَ, Korean 흩뜨리다). Nifal passive 'be scattered' is single occurrence. Isa 58:7 'break bread' (פָּרֹס) is specialized metaphorical extension — Arabic يَكْسِرَ/يُوَزِّعَ, Swahili -mega confirms food-distribution sense. 51→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7230","glossChirho":"רֹב multitude/abundance","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Abundance/multitude absorbs 43 of 56 clusters — fragmented purely by prepositional prefix, article, conjunction. 'Greatness/magnitude' separated by Spanish grandeza (not abundancia), Korean 큰 (big, not 많음), Swahili urefu (length, not wingi). Quantifier 'many/much' is adjectival use. Two verbal Qal forms caught (Qal Perf 3ms + Inf). 56→4."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0899b","glossChirho":"בֶּגֶד garment","sensesChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Fourth single-sense merge in project (after H0341, H0241, H6629). Initial concern about treachery homonym unfounded — this lemma covers only the noun. Arabic ثَوْب/ثِيَاب never خَانَ; verbal בגד 'act treacherously' is separate lemma (H0898 or H0899a). 63 clusters split entirely by suffix/prefix/number/article. 63→1."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0001","glossChirho":"אָב father","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Korean 아버지→조상 shift is diagnostic for father→ancestor split. Hindi पिता→पूर्वज/पितर confirms. 'Head of fathers' house' (בֵּית אָבוֹת) is institutional sense — Hindi पितृवंश (patriarchal lineage) distinguishes from both father and ancestor. Title of respect and metaphorical originator NOT split from core father sense — insufficient cluster evidence. 57→3."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8055","glossChirho":"שׂמח rejoice/be glad","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Clean binyan-based split: Qal intransitive 'rejoice' (57 clusters), Piel causative 'make glad' (19), single Hifil attestation (Ps 92:5). Arabic Form II فَرَّحَ vs Form IV أَفْرَحَ confirms Piel/Hifil distinction. Adjective שָׂמֵחַ kept separate per BDB. Infinitive construct separated for syntactic reasons. 82→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6086","glossChirho":"עֵץ tree/wood","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Arabic provides sharpest discrimination: شَجَرَة (living tree) vs خَشَب (timber) vs حَطَب (firewood) vs عَصَا (stick) vs عُود (shaft) — five distinct Arabic lexemes. Swahili mti vs mbao vs kuni vs fimbo reinforces. Korean 나무 ambiguous (covers tree+wood+timber). Gallows/stake (Esther) and flax-stalk (Josh 2:6) are singletons. 86→7."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5315","glossChirho":"נֶפֶשׁ soul/life/person","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Korean is primary discriminator: 영혼 (soul) vs 생명/목숨 (life) vs 사람 (person) vs 뜻/소원 (desire/will) vs 숨결 (breath) vs 생물 (creature). Swahili nafsi vs uhai vs mtu vs tamaa vs pumzi confirms. BDB/HALOT alignment: soul/life/creature/desire/person + breath. 57 of 100 clusters = soul/inner-being (dominant sense). Appetite/desire is clean split. 100→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2233","glossChirho":"זֶרַע seed/offspring","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Arabic نَسْل vs زَرْع is sharpest discriminator for offspring vs agricultural seed. 48 of 65 clusters = offspring/descendants (dominant covenant usage). Semen not separated — embedded in agricultural cluster, cannot isolate at cluster level. Sowing-time (Gen 8:22) caught via Korean 파종 and Swahili kupanda. 65→3."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2803","glossChirho":"חשׁב think/plan/reckon/weave","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Rich cognitive polysemy. Arabic فَكَّرَ (think) vs دَبَّرَ (scheme) vs حَسَبَ (reckon) vs قَصَدَ (intend) provides 4-way split of active senses. Exodus tabernacle craft sense (Arabic حائِكٍ weaver) clearly separate from abstract planning. Nifal passive absorbs both 'be counted' and 'be regarded' — Hebrew doesn't distinguish in passive. 95→7."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5060","glossChirho":"נגע touch/strike/reach","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Clean binyan mapping: Qal=touch (Arabic مَسَّ), Hifil=reach (Arabic بَلَغَ), Piel=strike (Arabic ضَرَبَ), Nifal/Pual=passive. Hifil causative 'bring upon' separated from intransitive 'reach'. Specialized Isa 5:8 'adjoin fields' is singleton extension. Prohibition 'don't touch' NOT split from general touch — same lexical sense, different pragmatics. 90→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7392","glossChirho":"רכב ride/mount","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Hifil splits into personal causative 'cause to ride' vs cargo 'load upon' — Korean 태우다 (person) vs 실다 (cargo), Swahili -pandisha vs -pakia are diagnostic. Qal participle 'rider/charioteer' substantivized per BDB. Divine epithet 'One who rides clouds' (Ps 68) absorbed into Qal participle. 55→4."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2603a","glossChirho":"חנן be gracious/show favor","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Hitpael 'implore favor' cleanly separated from Qal 'be gracious' — Arabic تَضَرَّعَ vs ارْحَمْ, Korean 간구하다 vs 은혜를 베풀다. Two distinct Piel senses: factitive 'make pleasant' (Arabic يُلَطِّفُ) vs intensive 'show compassion' (Arabic يَرحَمون). Disputed Nifal 'groan' (98082) may be separate root per BDB. 55→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7911","glossChirho":"שׁכח forget","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"49 of 66 clusters = Qal 'forget' (Arabic نَسِيَ, Korean 잊다). Nifal passive well-attested (11 occ). Piel and Hifil causative merged despite different stems — identical semantics (Arabic أَنْسَى). Single Hitpael (Eccl 8:10) kept separate — Arabic يَذْهَبُونَ suggests 'fade from memory' nuance. Participle and infinitive absolute separated per BDB. 66→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6912","glossChirho":"קבר bury","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Piel intensive/professional burial (Ezek 39 Gog mass interment) separated from Qal general burial. Piel participle הַקֹּבְרִים 'the buriers' is professional designation — Arabic الدَّافِنُون, Swahili wazikaji. Nifal/Pual/QalPass all merged as single passive sense (identical cross-linguistic profile). 56→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7227a","glossChirho":"רַב great/many/much","sensesChirho":10,"notesChirho":"Most senses of any lemma so far: 100→10. Arabic كَثِير vs عَظِيم vs رَئِيس vs كَفَى vs أَكْثَر provides 5-way split. Construct 'abounding in' is genuine adjectival sub-sense. Verbal forms (Qal 'increase') are separate POS. Title sense (chief/captain) may be homographic with Aramaic רַב. Singleton 'defender' likely from ריב root. 100→10."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6440","glossChirho":"פָּנִים face/before","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Massive grammaticalization: spatial לִפְנֵי (Arabic أَمَامَ) vs causal מִפְּנֵי (Arabic بِسَبَبِ) vs temporal לְפָנִים (Arabic قَبْلَ) — three completely different Arabic/Korean/Swahili words from same Hebrew noun. Cluster 127821 alone has ~1728 occurrences (spatial 'before'). Surface/face-of-earth kept separate (Korean 표면). 65→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4191","glossChirho":"מות die/kill/death","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Clean voice/causativity split: Qal die (Arabic مَاتَ) vs Hifil kill (Arabic قَتَلَ/أَمَاتَ) vs Hofal be-executed (Arabic يُقْتَلُ). מוֹת יוּמָת formula kept separate — emphatic legal/divine construction. Nominal participle 'the dead' (Arabic مَيِّت) and infinitive-as-noun 'death' (Arabic مَوْت) both substantivized. Piel/Polel intensive kill = 2 occ only. 89→7."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G1097","glossChirho":"γινώσκω know","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Could not reliably split experiential عَرَفَ from intellectual عَلِمَ — translators used both Arabic forms inconsistently within same clusters. 65 of 83 clusters = general 'know'. Passive 'be known' (8 clusters) cleanly separated by Arabic مَعْروف/عُرِفَ, Korean 알려지다. 'Understand' separated by Arabic فَهِمَ, Korean 깨닫다. Sexual 'know carnally' is singleton (Matt 1:25). 83→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3513","glossChirho":"כבד be heavy/honor/glory","sensesChirho":12,"notesChirho":"Most senses tied with H6965b (12). Arabic كَرَّمَ (honor person) vs مَجَّدَ (glorify God) is diagnostic Piel split — Korean 공경하다 vs 영화롭게 하다 confirms. Hifil 'harden heart' (Arabic أَثْقَلَ) vs Piel/Qal 'harden heart' kept separate by stem. Wealthy sense (Arabic غَنِيّ, Korean 부요했다) is genuine extension. Hitpael reflexive 'boast/multiply oneself' well-attested (3 occ). 92→12."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7604","glossChirho":"שׁאר remain/be left","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Clean Nifal/Hifil split: Arabic بَقِيَ (remain) vs أَبْقَى (leave remaining), Korean 남다 vs 남기다. Nifal participle 'remnant/survivors' substantivized per BDB — theologically significant prophetic term. 21 of 75 clusters = participle. All three senses have clean cross-linguistic separation. 75→3."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G0191","glossChirho":"ἀκούω hear/listen/obey","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"69 of 97 clusters = basic 'hear' (Arabic سَمِعَ). Swahili -sikiliza vs -sikia separates 'listen attentively' from 'hear'. Imperative/exhortatory 'heed/obey' (Korean 들으라) includes Synoptic 'he who has ears' formula. Passive 'be heard' (Arabic سُمِعَ, Korean 들리다, Swahili -sikika) is 7 clusters. Experiential→factual know split NOT attempted (unlike G1097). 97→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1870","glossChirho":"דֶּרֶךְ way/road/journey","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"56 of 82 clusters = metaphorical 'way of life/conduct' — dominant sense by cluster count. Swahili safari vs njia vs upande wa cleanly separates journey/road/direction. Arabic مَسِيرَة (journey-distance) vs طَرِيق (road) vs عَادَة (custom) provides 3-way split. Korean 길 is unfortunately ambiguous across most senses. Singleton 'direction' (Arabic نَحْوَ). 82→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0905","glossChirho":"בַּד alone/pole/linen","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Multiple homonymous roots collapsed under one lemma ID. Arabic وَحْدَهُ (alone) vs عصوين (staves) vs أَعْضَاء (limbs) vs فُرُوع (branches) vs قُضْبَان (bars) — 5 distinct Arabic lexemes. 'Apart from' (מִלְּבַד) is grammaticalized prepositional compound. Bars/bolts distinguished from poles/staves by Arabic مزاليج vs عصوين, Korean 빗장 vs 채. Linen sense NOT present in this data. 52→7."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6605a","glossChirho":"פתח open","sensesChirho":10,"notesChirho":"Arabic فَتَحَ (open) vs حَلَّ (loose) vs سَلَّ (draw sword) is primary 3-way split. Piel 'loose/untie' and 'set free person' are distinct extensions — Arabic فَكَّ/أَطْلَقَ, Korean 풀다/놓아주다. Open mouth/eyes separated from physical open per BDB. Draw sword (Arabic سَلَّ, Korean 빼다) is specialized military sense. Hitpael reflexive 'loose oneself' well-attested. 84→10."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2654a","glossChirho":"חפץ delight/desire","sensesChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Clean 2-way split: emotional pleasure (Arabic سُرَّ/رَضِيَ, Korean 기뻐하다) vs volitional desire (Arabic رَغِبَ/أُريدُ, Korean 원하다). Potential 3rd sense 'be willing/consent' (Arabic شَاءَ) collapsed into desire per BDB/HALOT. Most aggressive verb merge in recent batches: 53→2. Participial/adjectival absorbed into delight sense. 53→2."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5288","glossChirho":"נַעַר boy/youth/servant","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Arabic صَبِيٌّ (child) vs فَتًى/شَابّ (youth) vs غُلَام+possessive (servant) provides 3-way age/role split. Korean 아이 vs 젊은이 vs 종/하인 confirms. Temporal 'from youth' (מִנַּעַר) is grammaticalized expression. Armor-bearer and household attendant absorbed into servant sense. Feminine נַעֲרָה not in this lemma ID. 89→4."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7307","glossChirho":"רוּחַ spirit/wind/breath","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Arabic رُوح (spirit) vs رِيح (wind) — completely different Arabic words from different roots — is the most powerful discriminator. Swahili roho/upepo/pumzi provides independent 3-way confirmation. Korean 영/바람/숨 also confirms. Human spirit (27 clusters) vs divine Spirit (15) distinguished by context + Swahili capitalization. Spirit beings (أرواح broken plural) and direction (Arabic جِهَة, Korean 방향) are clean singletons. 84→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3027","glossChirho":"יָד hand/power/side","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"75 of 87 clusters = literal hand — massive inflectional fragmentation. Power/authority sense under-separated (only 1 cluster explicit; many power uses absorbed into literal hand — future token-level disambiguation needed). Side/bank (Arabic جَانِب) and monument (Hindi स्थान) are genuine BDB senses. Tabernacle tenon/peg (Hindi चूलें, Spanish espigas) is technical construction term. Idiomatic כְּיַד 'according to means' caught. 87→7."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6999a","glossChirho":"קטר burn incense/make smoke","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Piel 'burn incense' (Arabic بَخَّرَ) vs Hifil 'burn sacrifice' (Arabic أَوْقَدَ) is primary split — but Hifil also has incense-burning uses (Kings/Chronicles high places), kept as separate sense 3. SoS 3:6 Pual 'be perfumed' is genuine metaphorical extension. Single Hofal passive. 52→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5158a","glossChirho":"נַחַל wadi/brook/torrent","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Arabic وَادٍ (wadi) vs نَهْر (river) vs سَيْل (torrent) provides clean 3-way split. Valley/ravine (Korean 골짜기, Swahili bonde) separated from watercourse sense. Mining-shaft sense (Job 28:4) noted but cannot be isolated at cluster level — single occurrence mixed into wadi cluster. 63→4."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G2309","glossChirho":"θέλω want/wish/will","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Arabic أَرَادَ (want) vs شَاءَ (will/sovereign purpose) is key volitional split. 'Be willing/consent' (conative) separated from active 'want'. Optative/counterfactual 'wish' identified by Arabic كُنتُ أُريدُ (past continuous unfulfilled), Spanish subjunctive quisiera. 'Delight in' (Arabic الرّاغِبُ) is affective extension. 32 of 54 clusters = core 'want'. 54→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8269","glossChirho":"שַׂר prince/chief/official","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"48 of 76 clusters = broad 'prince/noble/official' sense. Military 'captain/commander' separated by Arabic قَائِد, Hindi सेनापति, Korean 지휘관. Construct 'chief-of-X' (cupbearer, baker, guard) is genuine pattern. Ruler/governor (Korean 통치자, 목백) separated from general prince. Angelic prince (Daniel) not distinguishable at cluster level. 76→4."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3201","glossChirho":"יכל be able/prevail","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Clean 3-way split matching BDB: ability (Arabic اسْتَطَاعَ, Korean 할 수 있다), prevail (Arabic غَلَبَ, Korean 이기다), endure (Arabic احْتَمَلَ, Korean 참다). 59 of 79 clusters = basic modal 'can/be able'. All three senses have strong cross-linguistic separation across all 6 target languages. 79→3."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7993","glossChirho":"שׁלך cast/throw/hurl","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Korean is key discriminator: 던지다 (throw) vs 버리다 (discard/reject) vs 내던지다 (fling down) vs 내쫓다 (expel). Hofal passive (12 clusters) cleanly separated by Arabic مَطْرُوح and Korean 던져지다. 'Cast away' metaphorical rejection (Ps 51:11) is genuine theological sense distinct from physical throwing. 84→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G0906","glossChirho":"βάλλω throw/cast/put","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Arabic وَضَعَ (put/place) vs أَلْقَى (cast/throw) is primary split — Korean 넣다 vs 던지다 confirms. 'Lie sick/prostrate' (Arabic مَطروح, Korean 누워 있다) is stative, not result of violent projection. 'Pour' (Arabic سَكَبَ, Korean 붓다) distinguished by liquid medium. Wind 'strike/beat' and 'cast out/expel' are singletons. 57→7."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8193","glossChirho":"שָׂפָה lip/edge/language","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Arabic شَفَة vs لُغَة vs حَافَة vs شَاطِئ vs الكَلَام — five distinct Arabic words. Korean 입술/언어/가장자리/해변/말 confirms 5-way split. Edge-of-object (manufactured) vs shore-of-water (natural) distinguished by Arabic حَافَة vs شَاطِئ. Speech sense is rare singleton. Gen 11 language sense is metonymic. 70→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0410","glossChirho":"אֵל God/god/power","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Arabic اللّٰه (definite, YHWH) vs إِلٰه (indefinite, generic god) vs قُوَّة (power/might) provides clean 3-way core split. Korean 하나님 vs 신 vs 힘 confirms. Compound divine names (El Shaddai etc.) absorbed into God-of-Israel. Oak/terebinth homonym caught (Spanish encinas, Korean 상수리나무들) — single cluster. El-in-proper-names distinguished by transliteration pattern. 53→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3947","glossChirho":"לקח take/receive/fetch","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"66 of 83 clusters = core 'take/seize' (Arabic أَخَذَ). 'Receive' (Arabic قَبُول, Korean 받다) vs 'fetch' (Arabic أَحْضِرْ, Korean 가져오다) are genuine BDB sub-senses. Gen 3:24 Hitpael 'flash/flame' (Arabic مُتَّقِدَة) is semantically unrelated homonym. Nifal/QalPass 'be taken' cleanly passive. 'Take away' singleton. 83→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5127","glossChirho":"נוס flee/escape","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"48 of 62 clusters = core Qal 'flee' (Arabic هَرَبَ). Substantival participle 'fugitive' (Arabic هَارِب, Spanish fugitivo) is 7 clusters. Hifil splits: causative 'put to flight' (Arabic أَهْرَبَ) vs 'hide/smuggle' (Arabic يُهَرِّبَ). Rare Hitpael 'rally/be lifted up' likely denominative from נֵס (banner) — all 6 languages render as 'lift up' not 'flee'. Metaphorical 'vanish' singleton. 62→7."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0251","glossChirho":"אָח brother/kinsman","sensesChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Most aggressive merge of a kinship term: 85→2. Arabic إِخْوَان (brethren/fellows) vs إِخْوَة (blood brothers) was ONLY cross-linguistic signal for broader 'kinsman' sense. Korean/Swahili did not differentiate. 73 of 85 clusters = literal brother. Reciprocal 'fellow/another' and 'political ally' senses not distinguishable at cluster level — would need contextual disambiguation. 85→2."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3925","glossChirho":"למד learn/teach","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Textbook binyan split: Qal learn vs Piel teach vs Pual trained/passive. Arabic تَعَلَّمَ (Form V middle) vs عَلَّمَ (Form II causative) mirrors the Hebrew Qal/Piel distinction perfectly — rare morphological isomorphism across Semitic families. Korean 배우다 vs 가르치다 confirms clean intransitive/transitive split. The substantivized Piel participle 'teacher' merits a separate sense due to nominal syntax with articles and suffixes."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5066","glossChirho":"נגשׁ draw near","sensesChirho":9,"notesChirho":"Full binyan-spectrum lemma with 5 stems attested: Qal (approach), Nifal (present oneself), Hifil (bring near/offer), Hofal (be offered), Hitpael (draw near together). Arabic قَرَّبَ (bring near) vs قَدَّمَ (present offering) distinguishes general Hifil from cultic Hifil. Korean 나아가다 (go forward) vs 다가가다 (approach) captures the advance vs approach distinction within Qal. The Nifal 'overtake' extension (curses/blessings reaching someone) is semantically distinct from spatial approach."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6963a","glossChirho":"קוֹל voice/sound","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Noun rather than verb, so semantic distinctions arise from referent rather than morphology. Arabic صَوْت covers all senses uniformly (human/divine/non-vocal), but Korean provides the key discriminator: 목소리 (human voice) vs 음성 (divine voice/formal speech) vs 소리 (sound/noise) vs 천둥 (thunder). The שׁמע בְּקוֹל idiom ('hear in the voice of' = obey) is grammaticalized enough to warrant its own sense. Thunder as plural קוֹלוֹת is lexicalized — Arabic رُعُود (thunders) and Hindi गड़गड़ाहटें (thunderclaps) confirm it as a distinct meaning."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5265","glossChirho":"נסע pull up/set out","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Classic etymological specialization: concrete 'pull up tent stakes' → abstract 'set out on journey'. Arabic رَحَلَ (journey) vs انْتَزَعَ (pull out) cleanly separates the two Qal senses. Korean 출발하다 (depart) vs 뽑아내다 (extract) confirms. Hifil splits into causative-of-journey (cause to depart) vs causative-of-pulling (uproot/quarry), with Arabic اقْتَلَعَ (uproot) vs رَحَّلَ (cause to journey) as the discriminator. The Nifal passive 'be uprooted' is metaphorical/prophetic."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3988a","glossChirho":"מאס reject/despise","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Arabic provides a 3-way lexical split within the Qal: رَفَضَ (rafada, active refusal) vs ازْدَرى (izdara, contemptuous scorn) vs سَئِمْتُ (sa'imtu, visceral loathing/weariness). These are three completely different Arabic roots mapping to the same Hebrew verb — strong evidence for genuine polysemy. Nifal passive cleanly separated by Arabic مَرْفُوض (passive participle) and Swahili -kataliwa. Korean 거부하다 (reject) vs 멸시하다 (despise) confirms the reject/scorn boundary."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0113","glossChirho":"אָדוֹן lord/master","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Arabic الرَّبّ (ar-rabb, the Lord/God) vs سَيِّد (sayyid, human lord) is the single most diagnostic feature for the divine vs human split. Korean 주 (ju, divine) vs 주인 (ju-in, human master) confirms. Plural lords warranted a separate sense due to Arabic broken plurals سَادَة/أَسْيَاد (distinct morphology). Owner-of-servants distinguished from respectful-address by Hindi मालिक (proprietor) vs स्वामी (lord) and dominant 3ms suffix pattern ('his master' in narrative)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5324","glossChirho":"נצב stand/set up","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Outstanding Arabic root discrimination: و-ق-ف (standing/posture) vs ن-ص-ب (erecting/placing) vs ث-ب-ت (firmness/establishment) maps three Arabic roots onto one Hebrew lemma, revealing polysemy invisible in the Hebrew alone. The substantivized Nifal participle 'officer/overseer' is a lexicalized administrative title — Arabic وَكِيل (deputy) and Korean 감독관 (supervisor) confirm this isn't just 'one who stands'. Hofal passive cleanly separated."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5159","glossChirho":"נַחֲלָה inheritance","sensesChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Minimal polysemy noun — 54 clusters were entirely due to grammatical fragmentation (pronominal suffixes, prepositions, number). Only 2 senses: concrete territorial inheritance vs metaphorical covenant heritage. Arabic مِيرَاث (mirath) used uniformly; Swahili urithi likewise. Spanish herencia vs heredad provides a slight signal for the concrete/abstract split. This is a case where the clustering algorithm badly over-split a semantically simple lexeme."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2896a","glossChirho":"טוֹב good","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Adjective with genuine polysemy beyond inflectional variation. Arabic خَيْرٌ (comparative 'better') vs حَسَنٌ (absolute 'good') cleanly separates comparative from absolute quality. The substantivized noun 'goodness' (Arabic الخَيْرَ) is a genuine POS shift. Aesthetic beauty sense ('fair of appearance') distinguished by Arabic جَمِيلَاتٌ, Korean 아름다운, Spanish hermosa. The 'glad/merry heart' (לֵב טוֹב) uses Arabic verbal طاب, confirming a stative-verbal sense distinct from adjectival."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7272","glossChirho":"רֶגֶל foot","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Classic body-part polysemy. The 'times' sense (שָׁלֹשׁ רְגָלִים) is confirmed by total lexical divergence in all 5 target languages — Arabic مَرَّات, Korean 번, Swahili mara, Hindi बार, Spanish veces — none using a foot-related word. The leg vs foot distinction uses Spanish pierna vs pie and Korean 다리 vs 발. 'Pace/step' (Arabic خُطوة, Hindi गति) and 'on foot' (Hindi पैदल) are genuine metonymic extensions. 91 of 99 clusters were purely grammatical fragmentation of the literal foot sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5826","glossChirho":"עזר help","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Semantically narrow verb — the core 'help' meaning dominates. The substantivized Qal participle 'helper' merits its own sense because all target languages use nominal/agentive forms: Arabic مُعِين, Korean 돕는 자, Swahili msaidizi, Hindi सहायक. The single Qal passive participle occurrence ('the helped one') shows distinct passive morphology. Nifal and Hifil provide expected voice/valence variation. Low polysemy — 53 clusters were overwhelmingly grammatical over-splitting."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7291","glossChirho":"רדף pursue","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Full binyan spectrum with Qal/Piel/Nifal/Hifil/Pual all attested. Within Qal, Arabic provides the key 3-way split: طارد (chase physically) vs اضطهد (persecute/oppress) vs اسعَ (seek/follow after). Korean 쫓다 (chase) vs 핍박 (persecute) vs 따르다 (follow) confirms. The abstract 'seek after peace/righteousness' sense is a genuine metaphorical extension. Piel intensive 'pursue relentlessly' distinguished by Arabic مُتَتَبِّع (persistent follower)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7130","glossChirho":"קֶרֶב midst/inward part","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Clean 3-sense noun matching BDB/HALOT exactly. Arabic provides the primary discriminator: وَسَط (wasaṭ, spatial midst) vs داخِل/قَلْب/باطِن (interior/heart/hidden nature) vs أَحْشَاء (viscera/entrails). Korean 가운데 (midst) vs 속 (inside) vs 내장 (intestines) confirms independently. The spatial→psychological→anatomical continuum is a classic body-interior polysemy chain. Levitical sacrificial contexts isolate the viscera sense cleanly."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4294","glossChirho":"מַטֶּה staff/tribe","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Classic concrete→abstract polysemy: staff/rod → scepter of authority → tribal identity. Arabic سِبْط (tribe) vs عَصَا (staff) is the primary discriminator — two completely different roots. Korean 지파 (tribe) vs 지팡이 (staff) confirms. Branch/shoot sense distinguished by Korean 가지, Swahili tawi. Divination rod as specialized implements — Arabic سِهَام (arrows), Swahili mishale."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7896","glossChirho":"שׁית set/place","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"All Qal stem, so binyan criterion didn't apply. Arabic provides the key polysemy evidence: وَضَعَ (spatial placement) vs جَعَلَ (causative-transformative 'make into') vs عَيَّنَ (appointment) vs وَجَّهَ (direction) — 4 different Arabic roots for one Hebrew verb within the same stem. Korean 놓다/두다 (place) vs 만들다 (make) vs 정하다 (appoint) confirms. Rare case where a single-stem verb has genuine 6-way polysemy rather than morphological splits."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2022","glossChirho":"הַר mountain","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Low polysemy geographic noun. Korean provides the key discriminator for the hill-country sense: 산 (mountain) vs 산지 (sanji, mountainous region/highlands). Hindi similarly: पहाड़ (mountain) vs पहाड़ी (pahaadi, hilly area). Arabic جَبَل uniformly covers all senses. The singular/plural split (mountain vs mountains) is borderline — could be collapsed but Arabic جَبَل/جِبَال broken plural morphology confirms conventional treatment as related but distinct senses."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2015","glossChirho":"הפך overturn/change","sensesChirho":8,"notesChirho":"Rich polysemy verb with full binyan coverage (Qal/Nifal/Hitpael/Hofal). Arabic provides 4-way Qal sub-split: قَلَبَ (overturn/destroy) vs حَوَّلَ (transform/change) vs انصرف/التفت (turn direction) vs حَرَّفَ (pervert/distort). Korean mirrors: 뒤집다 (flip) vs 바꾸다 (change) vs 돌아서다 (turn) vs 변하다 (be changed). The Sodom-overthrow meaning (Qal overturn) vs general transformation (Qal change) is a genuine polysemy split confirmed by all 5 target languages."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7812","glossChirho":"שׁחה bow down/worship","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Overwhelmingly Hitpael (71 of 73 clusters). The agent correctly unified worship-of-God and bow-before-king as contextual rather than lexical — Arabic سَجَدَ (sajada) used uniformly in both contexts. Qal imperative (1 occ) distinguished by Arabic انْحَنِي (inhani, bend/incline) — physical posture without devotional force. Hifil causative (1 occ) = 'cause to bow down'. The Hitpael dominance makes this one of the most lopsided sense distributions in the project."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7462b","glossChirho":"רעה shepherd/tend","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"The shepherd/tend/graze/devour semantic chain is a classic valency-based polysemy. Spanish provides the clearest transitive/intransitive split: apacentar (transitive tend) vs pacer/pastar (intransitive graze). Swahili -chunga (tend) vs -kula (eat) confirms. The substantivized participle 'shepherd' (34 clusters) is cleanly nominal across all languages — Arabic رَاعِي, Korean 목자, Swahili mchungaji. The destructive 'devour' extension (3 occ) is a genuine metaphorical sense, not just strong coloring of 'feed'."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0982","glossChirho":"בטח trust","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Arabic root distinction is the key discriminator: تَوَكَّلَ/وَثِقَ (active trust in someone) vs مُطْمَئِنّ (root ط-م-ن, stative feeling of security/ease). Two completely different Arabic roots map to one Hebrew Qal, revealing the active-trust vs stative-security polysemy. The stative sense covers both positive security and negative complacency (Isaiah's rebuke) — the difference is contextual. Hifil causative 'cause to trust' (5 occ) cleanly separated by binyan."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7931","glossChirho":"שׁכן dwell","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Qal splits 4 ways: dwell/inhabit (core), settle/rest-upon (Arabic لِيَحُلَّ, different root from سَكَنَ), be-inhabited (passive), and substantivized participle 'inhabitant' (Arabic سَاكِن nominal form). The Piel and Hifil are both causative but distinguished by morphology and by their Arabic forms. The 'settle/rest upon' sense (Arabic حَلَّ = alight/descend-upon) is semantically distinct from permanent dwelling — confirmed by Spanish reposar vs habitar."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5608a","glossChirho":"ספר count/tell","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Clean binyan-based split: Qal count / Nifal be counted / Piel narrate-tell / Pual be told. The Piel 'enumerate exhaustively' sub-sense is a semantic bridge between counting and narrating — Arabic أُحْصِي (root ح-ص-ي, exhaustive enumeration) vs أَخْبَرَ/حَدَّثَ (narrative roots). Despite English glosses varying between tell/recount/declare/proclaim, Arabic and Korean evidence does NOT separate these into distinct Piel senses — they are contextual nuances."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3240","glossChirho":"ינח cause to rest/leave","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Arabic provides a remarkable 4-way Hifil split: وَضَعَ (place/set down) vs تَرَكَ (leave/abandon) vs دَعَ (permit/let alone) vs طَرَحَ (cast down/throw) — four completely different Arabic roots for one Hebrew Hifil. The etymological core 'give rest/settle' (Arabic أَسْكَنَ/أَرَاحَ) is surprisingly rare (4 occ). Hofal passive cleanly separated. Sense 102540 correctly assigned to 'cast down' based on Arabic أُلْقِيكُمْ despite English gloss reading 'leave'."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7043","glossChirho":"קלל curse/be light","sensesChirho":12,"notesChirho":"Highest sense count this quad — 12 senses across 7 attested stems. The root q-l-l spans an extraordinary semantic range: be-light → be-swift → be-insignificant → curse (Piel intensive) → shake/agitate. Arabic provides clean splits: لعن (curse) vs خفّف (lighten burden) vs اهان (dishonor) vs رجّ (shake) vs حقير (insignificant). The Piel 'shake/agitate' (Arabic رجّ, Korean 흔들었다) is genuinely distinct from Piel 'curse' — zero translation overlap. Ties H3513 and H6965b for highest sense count."}
